DOMAIN: Structuralist and Post-Althusserian Interpretations
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-03-28
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 7, Medium: 5, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers Louis Althusser's structuralist reconstruction of Marxist
theory in the 1960s, its immediate legacy in the work of Etienne Balibar,
Nicos Poulantzas, and Jacques Ranciere, and the post-Althusserian
developments running from Althusser's own "aleatory materialism" of the 1980s
through to contemporary reassessments. The central intervention of "For Marx"
(1965) and "Reading Capital" (1965) was to read Marx as the founder of a
new science of social formations, distinct from the humanist-Hegelian problematic
of the early writings. The "epistemological break" thesis, borrowed from
Bachelard and Canguilhem, located this rupture circa 1845, producing concepts
irreducible to any philosophy of history: overdetermination (the displacement
of a Hegelian expressive totality by a complex, contradictory one), structural
causality (the absent cause operative only in its effects), and the mode of
production as a regional structure whose articulation does not conform to
any single temporality. Althusser's anti-humanism (rejecting the subject as
explanatory ground) and anti-historicism (rejecting History with a capital H
and teleology) placed him in opposition to both orthodox Soviet Marxism and
the Western Marxist tradition of Lukacs, Gramsci, and Sartre.

The immediate legacy split along several lines. Balibar's contribution to
"Reading Capital" theorized the articulation of modes of production and the
forms of transition between them, anticipating his later work on Marx's
philosophy more broadly. Poulantzas, working through Althusser's concepts
in "Political Power and Social Classes" (1968) and "State, Power, Socialism"
(1978), developed a structural Marxist theory of the state as a specific
condensation of class relations, increasingly attentive to democratic
conjunctures. The late Althusser's unpublished manuscripts introduced
"aleatory materialism" or the "materialism of the encounter," which abandoned
the scientism of the 1960s for a philosophy of contingency, chance, and
the void -- calling into question whether there are one or two Althussers.
Recent scholarship interrogates the continuity or discontinuity between the
structuralist and aleatory phases, Althusser's complex relationship to
Hegel and Spinoza, and the political stakes of reviving Althusserian
categories for contemporary Marxist theory.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
The Althusserian tradition is indispensable for a comprehensive survey of
interpretations of Marx's theory/philosophy of history because it represents
the most systematic attempt to establish historical materialism as a science
of social formations against both humanist and historicist readings. Its
specific theses about teleology, periodization, and the "process without a
subject or goal" articulate a distinctive anti-progressivist, anti-Hegelian
position that must be compared with Hegelian-Marxist, Western Marxist, and
analytical Marxist alternatives.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The reception of Althusser in Anglo-American analytical philosophy of history
remains understudied relative to French and continental reception. Balibar's
post-1990 work (on citizenship, the subject, the "vacillation of the
ideological") and its implications for the philosophy of history are
underrepresented in available verified secondary literature.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Contrast the Althusserian "process without a subject" with the humanist and
dialectical-Hegelian readings to bring out the stakes of the teleology and
historicism debates. The "aleatory turn" offers a useful bridge to discussions
of contingency and historical causation in other sections.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Structuralist Althusserianism (1960s): 7 papers -- epistemological break,
  structural causality, anti-humanism, anti-historicism
- Post-Althusserian developments: 4 papers -- Poulantzas on state, Balibar's
  later philosophy, post-structural Marxisms
- Aleatory materialism (late Althusser): 3 papers -- materialism of the
  encounter, contingency, nominalism, one vs two Althussers
====================================================================
}

@book{althusser1965formarx,
  author = {Althusser, Louis},
  title = {For Marx},
  publisher = {Verso},
  year = {1969},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Originally published in French as "Pour Marx" (1965), this
  collection of essays advances the "epistemological break" thesis: the mature
  Marx of Capital and the Grundrisse breaks entirely with the humanist,
  Feuerbachian problematic of the early writings, founding a new science
  of social formations irreducible to a philosophy of history. Key essays
  introduce the concepts of overdetermination (contradiction is always
  already overdetermined by other contradictions, denying Hegelian simple
  negation) and of the specific materialist dialectic that replaces the
  Hegelian model of expressive totality.

  RELEVANCE: Essential primary text for any account of Althusser's reading
  of Marx. Establishes the anti-historicist, anti-humanist framework that
  distinguishes his interpretation from Hegelian-Marxist, existentialist,
  and Western Marxist readings, all of which Althusser identifies as
  ideological rather than scientific.

  POSITION: Foundational statement of structuralist Althusserianism; introduces
  the epistemological break and overdetermination.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, structuralist-marxism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{althusserbalibar1965readingcapital,
  author = {Althusser, Louis and Balibar, Etienne},
  title = {Reading Capital},
  publisher = {Verso},
  year = {2016},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Originally published in French as "Lire le Capital" (1965),
  this collective work presents a "symptomatic reading" of Marx's Capital,
  attending to what the text leaves unsaid or answers through silences.
  Althusser develops the concept of structural causality -- the mode in which
  a structure is present in its effects without being reducible to them
  (contrasted with mechanistic and expressive causality). Balibar's
  contribution theorizes the articulation of modes of production, the concept
  of a social formation as a combination of several modes, and the problem of
  transition between modes.

  RELEVANCE: The other foundational primary text alongside "For Marx." Balibar's
  sections on modes of production and transition are directly relevant to
  discussions of periodization and historical change in Marx's theory of history.
  Structural causality is Althusser's answer to the question of how economic
  determination "in the last instance" operates without reducing to mechanism.

  POSITION: Core theoretical statement of the Reading Capital project;
  introduces structural causality, symptomatic reading, and mode of production
  theory.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, balibar, structural-causality, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{poulantzas1973politicalpower,
  author = {Poulantzas, Nicos},
  title = {Political Power and Social Classes},
  publisher = {Verso},
  year = {1973},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Poulantzas draws on Althusser's structural Marxism to develop
  a theory of political power and the capitalist state as a specific regional
  structure with relative autonomy from the economic. The state is not the
  direct instrument of a class but the organizing instance of the power bloc,
  functioning to maintain social cohesion and reproduce class domination. Classes
  are defined structurally (by their place in the relations of production) rather
  than by their consciousness or practices.

  RELEVANCE: The major Althusserian intervention into political theory and
  state theory. Raises the question of how Althusser's anti-humanism and
  structural concepts translate into analysis of concrete historical formations
  and political conjunctures -- a tension between structuralist theory and
  historical specificity that pervades Althusserian Marxism.

  POSITION: Althusserian structural Marxism applied to state theory; defines
  the post-Althusserian political theory tradition.
  },
  keywords = {poulantzas, state-theory, structuralist-marxism, High},
  abstract = {Diversos cientistas sociais que se dedicam à difícil tarefa de estudar o governo Lula da Silva têm retomado a reflexão sobre o período desenvolvimentista brasileiro. Neste artigo, examinamos as relações entre poder político, regime político e política de Estado durante: 1) o apogeu e crise final do nacional&#8209;desenvolvimentismo; 2) o neonacional&#8209;desenvolvimentismo. Nosso objetivo é contribuir para a análise de dois importantes momentos do Brasil contemporâneo.<br>Several social scientists engaged in the difficult task of studying Lula da Silva's government have resumed the reflection on Brazilian developmentalist period. In this article, we examine the relationships among political power, political regime and State policy during: 1) the rise and final crisis of national developmentalism; 2) the neo&#8209;national developmentalism. Our purpose is to contribute to the analysis of two important moments of contemporary Brazil},
  abstract_source = {core},
}

@book{poulantzas1978statepowersocialism,
  author = {Poulantzas, Nicos},
  title = {State, Power, Socialism},
  publisher = {Verso},
  year = {1980},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Poulantzas's final major work revises his earlier structural
  Marxism in the direction of a more relational theory of power (influenced by
  Foucault) and a "democratic road to socialism." The state is reconceived not
  as a monolithic apparatus but as a "material condensation of a relationship
  of forces among classes and class fractions." This move distances Poulantzas
  from strict Althusserian structuralism while retaining the emphasis on class
  relations and structural determination.

  RELEVANCE: Marks the post-Althusserian evolution of state theory, responding
  to critiques that Althusser's framework was too rigid to capture historical
  variation and political transformation. Shows how the transition problem
  appears not only at the level of modes of production but in the theory of the
  state and political change.

  POSITION: Post-Althusserian development; integrates structural Marxism with
  relational power analysis.
  },
  keywords = {poulantzas, state-theory, post-althusserian, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{sotiris2014structure,
  author = {Sotiris, Panagiotis},
  title = {Rethinking Structure and Conjuncture in Althusser},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {22},
  number = {3-4},
  pages = {5--51},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-12341344},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Against the standard narrative that Althusser first embraced
  and then abandoned structuralism, Sotiris argues that Althusser consistently
  tried to articulate structural causality with the irreducible singularity of
  historical conjunctures. The concept of "overdetermination" already implies
  that structures are always conjunctural -- never fully closed or self-identical.
  Sotiris proposes this as a productive starting point for a non-dualist
  materialist theory of the social that avoids both deep-structure determinism
  and empiricist conjuncturalism.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses how the Althusserian tradition handles the
  relation between structural determination and historical specificity, which
  is central to debates about Marx's philosophy of history. Rehabilitates
  Althusser's framework for thinking periodization and transition without
  teleology or essentialism.

  POSITION: Revisionist defense of Althusserian structuralism; argues for the
  constitutive role of conjuncture within structural theory.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, structural-causality, conjuncture, High},
  abstract = {The relation between structure and conjuncture has been one of the biggest challenges facing social theory and Louis Althusser’s writings provide some of the most important interventions on this subject. Contrary to an image of Althusser first embracing and then abandoning structuralism, Althusser tried from the beginning to articulate the theory of structural causality with an insistence on the singularity of historical conjunctures. Althusser’s theoretical trajectory, despite his shortcomings, still offers a necessary starting point for a materialist conception of the relation between structural and conjunctural determinations that stresses the complex, uneven and overdetermined character of social reality without resorting either to a ‘surface phenomena/deep structures’ dualism or to the empiricism of simply registering singular practices.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{maher2016structuralism,
  author = {Maher, Stephen},
  title = {Escaping Structuralism's Legacy: The Renewal of Theory and History in Historical Materialism},
  journal = {Science & Society},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {80},
  number = {3},
  pages = {291--318},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.2016.80.3.291},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Maher argues that Althusserian structuralism left historical
  materialism with a theoretical legacy that rendered genuine historical
  analysis difficult, by subordinating contingent processes to structural
  invariants. Drawing on more recent theoretical developments in historical
  sociology and critical political economy, the essay proposes ways to renew
  historical materialism by preserving structural analysis while reintegrating
  the dimension of historical agency and conjunctural specificity.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a critical external assessment of the Althusserian
  legacy from within the historical materialism tradition, useful for
  contextualizing the strengths and limits of the structural Marxist approach
  to Marx's philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Immanent critique of Althusserian structuralism; calls for
  renewal of historical materialism beyond the structuralist impasse.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, historical-materialism, structuralism-critique, Medium},
  abstract = {The project of renewing historical materialist theory for a new generation requires overcoming the limitations of Althusserian structuralism. Upon investigation, Althusser's theory is revealed to be idealist, inconsistent with the philosophy actually articulated by Marx, and unhelpful in understanding the dynamics of human societies. The flaws in this approach led both to E. P. Thompson's rejection of abstract theory altogether, and Michael Lebowitz's effort to “complete” Marx's theoretical project, while avoiding Althusser's structural determinism by incorporating agency and social struggle into the heart of political economy. Yet Lebowitz's theoretical system has very little to say about how these were historically practiced by classes as institutional formations in different spatio-temporal contexts. In this respect, the “Institutional Marxist” approach developed by Leo Panitch and Sam Gindin shows the most promise for further developing today's revival of historical materialism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{montag2010lateAlthusser,
  author = {Montag, Warren},
  title = {The Late Althusser: Materialism of the Encounter or Philosophy of Nothing?},
  journal = {Culture, Theory and Critique},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {51},
  number = {2},
  pages = {157--170},
  doi = {10.1080/14735784.2010.496593},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Montag critically examines Althusser's late "materialism of
  the encounter" (aleatory materialism), asking whether it represents a genuine
  philosophical advance beyond the structuralism of the 1960s or whether it
  dissolves Marxist science into a philosophy of pure contingency. He argues
  that the late texts risk evacuating the structural determinations necessary
  for political analysis, reducing materialism to a celebration of the void
  and the aleatory.

  RELEVANCE: Key critical engagement with the aleatory turn that raises the
  question of whether Althusser's late work represents a continuation or
  a rupture with the structuralist project, with implications for how his
  treatment of historical causation and teleology should be evaluated.

  POSITION: Critical of aleatory materialism; defends the need for structural
  causality against its dissolution into pure contingency.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, aleatory-materialism, late-althusser, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract The ‘late Althusser,’ above all the posthumously published ‘Underground Current of the Materialism of the Encounter,’ is often regarded as a ‘break’ with his earlier work. The late works are read as a rejection of the ‘determinism’ supposedly characteristic of such texts as For Marx and Reading Capital. This essay seeks to show in contrast that a ‘materialism of the encounter’ is at work in the early texts, and that what is new in the late works is a return to a philosophy of origins, of an originary void as the guarantee that all that exists will pass away. There is thus a Messianism which remains the unthought residue of the late Althusser and which calls for analysis.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{erdem2019hegelrelation,
  author = {Erdem, Erdinc},
  title = {Althusser's Relation to Hegel: French Hegelianism, Marxism, and Aleatory Materialism},
  journal = {Rethinking Marxism},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {31},
  number = {3},
  pages = {380--401},
  doi = {10.1080/08935696.2019.1629198},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: By tracing Althusser's engagement with Hegel across his career,
  Erdem shows that the relationship is more complicated than Althusser's
  programmatic anti-Hegelianism suggests. In the posthumous writings, two
  Althusserian notions -- "philosophy as class struggle in theory" and "history
  as a process without a subject" -- emerge from encounters with Hegel rather
  than simple rejection, challenging the clean break narrative.

  RELEVANCE: Important for understanding the precise character of Althusser's
  anti-historicism and how his critique of Hegelian teleology actually works.
  The claim that "history is a process without a subject or goal" is a direct
  response to Hegel's philosophy of history, and tracing this response matters
  for the broader project of mapping interpretations of Marx's theory of history.

  POSITION: Complicates the standard anti-Hegelian narrative; shows the
  dialectical character of Althusser's rejection of Hegelianism.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, hegel, anti-historicism, Medium},
  abstract = {This essay concerns the uneasy relationship between Althusser and Hegel that can be observed throughout Althusser’s oeuvre. In the 1960s Althusser pursued a rigorous project and tried to develop a systematic account of philosophy, history, and science from within Marxism—an account that would constitute a theoretical weapon against Hegelian idealism. Althusser’s arguments in For Marx and Reading “Capital” established his presence in French philosophical corners as a Marxist and anti-Hegelian thinker. However, in posthumous publications, Althusser’s relation to Hegel appears far more complicated than his simply being anti-Hegelian. By investigating this relationship from the lens of multiple encounters, this essay carves out two notions that emerged from these encounters, notions that we call “Althusserian”: philosophy as class struggle in theory and history as a process without a subject.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{goshgarian2015marxistphilosophy,
  author = {Goshgarian, G. M.},
  title = {A Marxist in Philosophy},
  journal = {Diacritics},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {43},
  number = {2},
  pages = {24--46},
  doi = {10.1353/dia.2015.0012},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Goshgarian, the principal translator of Althusser's late
  writings, examines the development of Althusser's self-understanding as a
  philosopher working within Marxism. Drawing on unpublished manuscripts, he
  shows how Althusser increasingly located philosophy not as a science alongside
  historical materialism, but as a practice of taking sides in ideological
  class struggle -- a self-revision with consequences for how the scientific
  status claims of "For Marx" and "Reading Capital" should be read.

  RELEVANCE: Provides insight into the internal evolution of Althusser's
  philosophical project from archival perspective, relevant to assessing
  the coherence of structuralist claims about historical materialism as a
  science and their modification in the later period.

  POSITION: Archival and biographical perspective on Althusser's philosophical
  development; documents self-revision of the scientism of the 1960s.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, late-althusser, philosophy, Medium},
  abstract = {This lightly modified version of the editor’s introduction to Être marxiste en philosophie situates that 1976 text with respect to the aleatory materialism or materialism of the encounter that Althusser began to elaborate in the late 1950s; the theory of class dictatorship that he consistently took to be central to Marxism; and the deconstructive anti-philosophy that he espoused in the 1950s and again from his anti-theoreticist turn of 1966–67 on. The central stake of this turn was the elaboration of a theory of Marxist non-philosophy compatible with a materialism of the encounter: Althusser grounds it, in Être marxiste , on the concept of the “non-state” at the core of the concept of the dictatorship of the proletariat—unsurprisingly, because his understanding of the way a class dictatorship crystallizes (or fails to) out of the destruction of another doubtless provided the starting point for his elaboration of the materialism of the encounter. Whence a conceptualization of philosophy as an encounter—the encounter of class struggle—at the level of theory. Whence also a redefinition of the stakes of theoretical class struggle, of which the only significant record is Althusser’s newly published work of the mid-1970s, aside from an inconspicuous footnote in Reply to John Lewis . If Althusser had previously located the primary objective of philosophical-political battle in the social destiny of the sciences, he now broadens it to take in the destiny of all the social practices and the corresponding ideologies, whose domestication in the interests of the exploiting classes or liberation in the interests of the exploited is, he now says, what philosophical argument is ultimately about. “A Marxist in Philosophy” proposes to show how, in the mid-1970s, these elements gelled in a new configuration that gave rise to Althusser’s well-known aleatory-materialist writings of the mid-1980s.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{morfino2021twoaleatories,
  author = {Morfino, Vittorio},
  title = {Are there One or Two Aleatory Materialisms?},
  booktitle = {Cultural Inquiry},
  publisher = {ICI Berlin Press},
  year = {2021},
  pages = {91--106},
  doi = {10.37050/ci-20_05},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Morfino revisits his earlier interpretation that aleatory
  materialism can be traced back to the 1960s, identifying two distinct
  tendencies within the late Althusserian texts: a genuinely materialist
  tendency (focused on the concept of encounter and contingency) and an
  eschatological tendency that emerged in the late 1970s and which risks
  reintroducing a form of teleology at the level of political hope. This
  reading argues for the need to separate these strands for productive
  appropriation.

  RELEVANCE: Directly relevant to the question of whether Althusser's late
  work offers resources for a non-teleological philosophy of history or whether
  it reintroduces teleology through the back door. The continuity/discontinuity
  question between the two phases of Althusser's thought maps onto the broader
  project's concern with teleology and anti-teleological Marxism.

  POSITION: Immanent differentiation within aleatory materialism; distinguishes
  genuine contingency-thinking from eschatological residue.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, aleatory-materialism, teleology, Medium},
  abstract = {The chapter will consider the continuity of Althusser’s thought and revise my previous interpretation backdating aleatory materialism to the sixties. I will analyse the context in which the concept of ‘encounter’ emerged in the sixties and show in a second moment that it is possible to identify this concept in the texts of the eighties, but only in one of the two tendencies which traverses this group of texts, the one I would call a materialist tendency. This tendency is intertwined with another tendency, an eschatological one — which emerged in the late seventies.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{carmichael2022aestheticaleatory,
  author = {Carmichael, Thomas},
  title = {'[A]nother kind of rain': Aesthetic Ontology and Contagious Imaginations in Althusser's Aleatory Materialism},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {30},
  number = {3},
  pages = {178--205},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-20222064},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carmichael argues that Althusser's cultural criticism has been
  neglected in discussions of aleatory materialism, and that tracing the genealogy
  of the aleatory impulse through Althusser's aesthetic writings illuminates
  both the logic of the late texts (including the larger unpublished manuscript
  from which "The Underground Current of the Materialism of the Encounter" was
  extracted) and the significance of Malebranche for Althusser's philosophical
  development.

  RELEVANCE: By situating aleatory materialism in relation to Althusser's
  aesthetics and his reading of specific philosophical sources, this paper
  contributes to understanding the intellectual genealogy of the late philosophy
  and its departure from the structuralist framework of the 1960s, relevant
  to tracing the development of Althusser's anti-teleological thought.

  POSITION: Contextualizes aleatory materialism within Althusser's broader
  intellectual development; highlights the aesthetic dimension.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, aleatory-materialism, aesthetics, Low},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
One of the remarkable features of recent interest in the work of Louis Althusser has been the prominence granted to Althusser’s cultural criticism. However, aside from one notable exception, Althusser’s cultural criticism has not figured prominently in discussions of his late work, and the absence of Althusser’s cultural criticism from those discussions has perhaps unwittingly obscured both the genealogy of Althusser’s late aesthetic ontology and the logic of his last texts, of ‘The Underground Current of the Materialism of the Encounter’ and related writings on aleatory materialism. By tracing the genealogy of the aleatory impulse in Althusser’s cultural criticism, it is possible to show how that work resonates in Althusser’s late texts including the larger unpublished manuscript from which ‘The Underground Current of the Materialism of the Encounter’ has been drawn. This reading of the philosophy of the encounter also allows us to understand the importance of Nicolas Malebranche for Althusser’s aleatory materialism.},
}

@article{kozluca2021postAlthusserian,
  author = {Kozluca, Eren},
  title = {Theoretical Acupunctures: From Althusser to the Post-Althusserian Marxism of Robert Linhart},
  journal = {Rethinking Marxism},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {33},
  number = {4},
  pages = {533--562},
  doi = {10.1080/08935696.2021.1972669},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Starting from the limitations of Althusser's combined project
  of "concrete analysis" and aleatory materialism, Kozluca examines the original
  post-Althusserian Marxism of Robert Linhart, arguing that Linhart's work on
  productive labor, finitude, and normativity extends and corrects Althusserian
  concepts in ways useful for Marxian social research. Linhart's attention to
  the materiality of subjective experience and aleatory singularity offers an
  operationalization of concepts that Althusser left at the level of abstract
  philosophy.

  RELEVANCE: Useful for understanding how Althusserian categories were extended
  and modified in the generation immediately following, specifically in relation
  to the question of how structural analysis and singular historical cases
  can be articulated -- a key issue for any account of Marx's philosophy of
  history in the Althusserian tradition.

  POSITION: Post-Althusserian development; extends the framework toward
  concrete Marxian social research.
  },
  keywords = {post-althusserian, linhart, structural-causality, Low},
  abstract = {Departing from an epistemological investigation into the limitations of the conjoined Althusserian problematic of concrete analysis and aleatory materialism, this essay aims to circumscribe the original features and possible present contributions of Robert Linhart's Marxism, arguing that, thanks to an enriched “philosophical conception of productive labor” preoccupied with a materialist understanding of the categories of finitude and normativity, Linhart's texts of the late 1970s and early 1980s offer fresh insights into the question of how subjective experiences, singularly aleatory cases, and properly experimental setups should be articulated. The essay claims that Linhart's Marxism displays the characteristics of an original post-Althusserian enterprise whose ultimate relevance lies in its interest in the adequate extension, operationalization, and rectification of concepts involved in Marxian social research.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{hinton2024nominalism,
  author = {Hinton, Timothy},
  title = {Nominalism, materialism, and history},
  journal = {Philosophy & Social Criticism},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {52},
  number = {1},
  pages = {169--188},
  doi = {10.1177/01914537241234692},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hinton addresses two unresolved gaps in Althusser's late work:
  the relationship between nominalism and materialism, and the relationship
  between aleatory materialism and a broadly materialist account of history.
  Drawing on Hobbes's nominalism and Deleuze's concept of the plane of immanence,
  he develops the concept of an "aleatory causal chain" that can be operative
  in human history without invoking teleology or essentialism.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most systematic recent attempt to think through how
  Althusser's aleatory materialism can ground an account of historical causation,
  directly relevant to the project's central concern with teleology and historical
  explanation in Marx's framework.

  POSITION: Constructive engagement with late Althusser; develops aleatory
  causation as a resource for non-teleological philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {althusser, aleatory-materialism, historical-causation, Medium},
  abstract = {This article addresses two explanatory gaps in Althusser’s late work. One has to do with the relation between nominalism and materialism; the other engages the relation between Althusser’s later materialism and a broadly materialist approach to history. In the first part of the article, I develop a response to the problem of nominalism that makes use of Hobbes’s nominalism and Deleuze’s concept of the plane of immanence. In the second part, I address the problem of history by explaining the concept of an aleatory causal chain, and showing how such chains could be at work in human history. I also make use of Hobbes’s materialist account of causation, applying it to social relations, social collectivities, and historical events.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{diefenbach2013encountering,
  author = {Diefenbach, Katja and Farris, Sara R. and Kirn, Gal and Thomas, P.},
  title = {Encountering Althusser: Politics and Materialism in Contemporary Radical Thought},
